<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>Taylor series</title>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>Starting with the geometric series
  <md>
  \frac1{1-x} = 1+x+x^2+x^3+\ldots+x^n+\ldots,
  </md>
  find the Taylor series for <m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{1+u^2}</m> by making
  the substitution <m>x=-u^2</m>.  Be sure to write an expression for the
  general term.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Use this to find the Taylor series, including an expression for the
  general term, for
  <md>
  \arctan(x) = \int_0^x \frac1{1+u^2}~du
  </md></p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Write the series for <m>\arctan(1)</m>.  How many terms would you need to
  estimate this with an error less than 0.001?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Write the series for <m>\arctan(0.5)</m>.  How many terms would you need
  to estimate this with an error less than 0.001?  Go ahead and
  evaluate your sum and verify that its error, the amount that it
  differs from <m>\arctan(0.5)</m> is less than 0.001.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Use the Taylor series for <m>\cos(x)</m> to estimate <m>\cos(0.5)</m> with
  an error less than 0.0001.  Explain how you know the error satisfies
  this condition.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose you need to evaluate the integral <m>\int_0^1
  x\sin(x^3)~dx</m>.  Start wth the series representation of <m>\sin(u)</m> to
  obtain the series representation of <m>\sin(x^3)</m>, then multiply by
  <m>x</m> and integrate.  Find the general term, determine how many
  terms you would need to estimate the integral with an error less
  than <m>0.0001</m>, then form the estimate.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Using the definition of the factorial, explain why <m>\frac{5}{5!}
  = \frac{1}{4!}</m> and why <m>\frac{10}{10!} = \frac{1}{9!}</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>We have seen the Taylor series
  <md>
  e^x = 1+x+\frac1{2!}x^2+\frac1{3!}x^3 + \frac1{4!}x^4 + \ldots +
  \frac{1}{n!}x^n + \ldots.
  </md>
  Differentiate the Taylor series of <m>e^x</m> and explain why
  <m>\frac{d}{dx}e^x = e^x</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
